=============================================================
NUMPY TOPIC 2 — INDEXING AND SLICING
Revision Report
=============================================================

CORE PURPOSE
------------
Indexing and slicing let you select, extract, and mutate specific
elements, rows, columns, and submatrices without loops. In ML
pipelines this covers train/test splits, feature selection, batch
extraction, and sequence windowing. Understanding views vs copies
is non-negotiable — silent mutations through views are one of the
most common sources of hard-to-trace bugs in data pipelines.

KEY SYNTAX
----------
a[i]              # single element (scalar)
a[i:j]            # slice from i to j-1 (stop exclusive)
a[i:j:k]          # slice with step k
a[::-1]           # reverse along axis 0
a[-1]             # last element
a[-3:]            # last 3 elements

2D:
A[i, j]           # single element at row i, col j
A[i, :]           # row i — explicit form, preferred
A[:, j]           # column j
A[i:j, k:l]       # submatrix
A[-3:, -4:]       # last 3 rows, last 4 cols
A[::-1]           # reverse rows
A[:, ::-1]        # reverse columns
A[i][j]           # works but slower — creates intermediate array

VIEWS VS COPIES
---------------
Basic slicing → VIEW (shares memory with original)
  b = a[1:4]          # b and a share memory
  b[0] = 99           # mutates a through b

.copy() → independent copy
  b = a[1:4].copy()   # b is independent
  b[0] = 99           # a unchanged

np.shares_memory(a, b)  # True if shared, False if copy

Rule of thumb:
  Basic slice (start:stop:step)  → view
  Fancy indexing (list/array)    → always a copy (covered fully in Topic 6)

AXIS INTUITION
--------------
axis 0 → moves down rows (collapses rows)
axis 1 → moves across columns (collapses columns)

A.sum(axis=0)   # one value per column — shape (n_cols,)
A.sum(axis=1)   # one value per row    — shape (n_rows,)

For 3D: axis 0=depth, axis 1=rows, axis 2=cols
images[:, 0, :]  # top row of every image in a batch

PATTERNS
--------
# Train/test split
X_train = X[:n_train]
X_test  = X[n_train:]

# Feature column extraction (view)
col = X[:, j]

# Multi-column selection (copy — fancy indexing)
X_selected = X[:, [0, 3, 5, 7]]    # shape (n_samples, 4)

# Every other sample
X_sampled = X[::2]

# Bottom-right submatrix (robust to shape changes)
sub = X[-m:, -n:]

# Reverse rows / columns
X[::-1]          # row reversal
X[:, ::-1]       # column reversal

GOTCHAS
-------
1. Slice stop is always exclusive
   data[2:4] → rows 2, 3 only (row 4 excluded)
   data[2:5] → rows 2, 3, 4

2. .copy without () is silent — references the method, doesn't call it
   last_rows = data[-3:].copy    # last_rows is a method object
   last_rows = data[-3:].copy()  # correct

3. Stacking columns with np.array() transposes the result
   np.array([X[:, 0], X[:, 3]])  # shape (2, n_samples) — WRONG
   X[:, [0, 3]]                  # shape (n_samples, 2) — correct

4. A[i][j] vs A[i, j]
   Both return the same value but A[i][j] creates an intermediate
   array for the first index — slower and doesn't generalise to
   higher-dimensional slicing

5. Whitespace inside index brackets is legal but inconsistent style
   data[: ,0] works — a reviewer will clean it to data[:, 0]

6. Negative indices are more robust than hardcoded positional ones
   X[3:6, 3:6]  → breaks if shape changes
   X[-3:, -3:]  → always means "last 3 rows, last 3 cols"

WHEN TO USE .copy()
-------------------
- When you need to modify a slice without affecting the original
- When passing a slice to a function that may mutate it
- When storing a slice that outlives the original array
- When you explicitly want to confirm independence (+ np.shares_memory)

